{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 40, :comments ["<b>And he who eats of its carcass.</b> The verse speaks of the carcass of a clean bird, which does not defile by contact; but an animal defiles by contact, while a clean bird does not defile by contact, but it does defile garments in the house of swallowing, that is, his garments receive impurity at the moment he swallows it. And according to this, when it says 'from the animal,' it includes also the bird, and there is no need to mention the wild animal, for it has already been clarified that חיה is included in בהמה and בהמה is included in חיה." "<b>And do not</b> be astonished at the tradition of our Rabbis, of blessed memory, who interpreted that the verse speaks of the carcass of a clean bird and who understood bird as included in animal — for we find (Leviticus 24:21): ״ומכה בהמה ישלמנה״, and surely the bird too is included, for thus reason obligates that anyone who damages a thing must pay restitution; but the way of the verses is to speak and to specify what is more common. And likewise it is written (Deuteronomy 5:14): ״ושורך וחמורך וכל בהמתך״, and surely the bird too is included, for they forbade him to fly his doves on the Sabbath. Also in the matter of a deposit we find written (Exodus 22:9): ״וכל בהמה לשמור״."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:24:55.335872863Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}